INT. LAUNDROMAT-TURNED-TEAHOUSE - CHINATOWN BASEMENT - EVENING

Steam. Paper lanterns. A row of dormant washing machines repurposed as tables, round windows full of potted jasmine. A tea set on each.

BEATRIX LAU, 40s, in a high-collared jacket and wire glasses, pours oolong at a corner table. Across from her: a DELIVERY MAN, 30s, in a rain-soaked windbreaker, cap low, a flat cardboard box beside his chair.

BEATRIX
You're early.

DELIVERY MAN
Traffic was light.

BEATRIX
In this city?

DELIVERY MAN
I took the back streets.

She slides a cup across. He takes it with both hands. Sips. Eyes closed.

DELIVERY MAN (CONT'D)
That's very good.

BEATRIX
My grandmother's blend.

DELIVERY MAN
She taught you?

BEATRIX
She taught everyone. Whether they wanted lessons or not.

He laughs. She smiles, small, practiced. At the bar, CASSIUS, 60s, silver beard, an old man in a tweed vest, stirs sugar into a glass and watches them in the mirror.

DELIVERY MAN
Ms. Lau, about the package.

BEATRIX
Let's finish our tea.

DELIVERY MAN
Of course.

They sip. The jasmine nods in the steam.

BEATRIX
Do you have family?

DELIVERY MAN
A brother in Oakland.

BEATRIX
Do you call him?

DELIVERY MAN
Sundays.

BEATRIX
Good. A man should call his brother.

She sets her cup down. Taps the rim twice. At the bar, Cassius puts down his glass and turns on his stool.

CASSIUS
Beatrix. Is this the one?

BEATRIX
Yes, Cassius.

DELIVERY MAN
(lifting the cap, eyes widening)
What is this?

BEATRIX
Please. Keep your voice low. Your name is Malcolm Reyes.

MALCOLM
How do you know that?

BEATRIX
Because I paid for your last six months of silence.

MALCOLM
You? That was you?

CASSIUS
(approaching)
It was both of us.

MALCOLM
Oh, God.

BEATRIX
Malcolm, the box. Open it.

MALCOLM
I'd rather not.

BEATRIX
I know.

He fumbles with the lid. Inside, nested in tissue paper, a single white teacup and a folded photograph.

MALCOLM
This isn't what they told me to deliver.

BEATRIX
No. It's what you were meant to find.

She turns the photo toward him. A young man at a laundromat counter, a younger Malcolm, smiling, with a woman at his side. His mother.

MALCOLM
Where did you get this?

CASSIUS
She kept the shop. Before it was this.

MALCOLM
She was my... she's dead. They said she was dead.

BEATRIX
She died. Not the way they told you.

Malcolm sits very still. The steam rises between them, soft and sweet.

CASSIUS
Drink your tea, son.

Malcolm lifts the cup to his lips, hand trembling. He stops. Looks at Beatrix.

MALCOLM
Is it poisoned?

BEATRIX
No. But I wanted you to wonder.

He drinks anyway.
